WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.080
This is the first staged photo.

00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:05.140
It depicts a suicide.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.980
But it also tells a story.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.680
Of an ambitious amateur inventor who found
a way to achieve the incredible.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:17.400
But was pushed aside by a powerful, meddling
politician with his own agenda.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.380
And doomed to obscurity.

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.080
Hippolyte Bayard was an artist.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:29.800
A gardener.

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:30.980
A collector.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:32.800
And an experimenter.

00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:37.840
And in 1839, he invented photography – sort
of.

00:00:38.340 --> 00:00:41.640
People knew for centuries how to create an
image with light, but they didn’t have a

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:44.320
way to permanently fix it onto a surface.

00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.740
Until several people actually figured it out
all at once.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.260
They invented their own methods, building
on years of individual experimentation and

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:53.460
failed attempts.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.960
Bayard, in France, was one of them.

00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.528
He was experimenting with photography in his
free time while working a day job

00:00:59.528 --> 00:01:01.080
in a government office.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.180
He found a way to fix images by dipping paper
into photosensitive chemicals and exposing

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.640
it to light inside a camera obscura, which
is essentially a dark room with a tiny hole

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:11.300
in it.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:16.960
You can actually see stains from excess chemicals
in his earliest successful attempts.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.689
His photographs prominently featured his home
and garden,

00:01:19.689 --> 00:01:24.680
where he would arrange tools, statues,
and vases into loaded still lifes.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:25.940
Also windmills.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.140
He loved windmills.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.380
Often he included himself in the scenes, eyes
closed because of the long exposure time his

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:33.700
process required.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:37.090
But he’s not the photo pioneer you might
have heard of.

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.100
That would be this guy: fellow Frenchman Louis
Daguerre.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.580
Daguerre had already made a name for himself
and captured the public’s attention in the

00:01:44.590 --> 00:01:49.409
1820s with popular interactive art shows,
called “dioramas.”

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.299
Fixing an image to a surface was his next
big ambition.

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:56.850
And by the late 1830s, he’d had his own
breakthrough with photography, successfully

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.959
creating a process to fix an image to a metal
plate.

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:02.340
He called his invention the Daguerreotype.

00:02:03.170 --> 00:02:07.780
He kept his discovery mostly under wraps,
showing the Daguerreotypes to just a few influential

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:12.300
members of society, who would then spread
rumors of his success to the press.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:17.000
Bayard, on the other hand, worked alone, but
he was eager to share his work.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:22.860
In June 1839 he held a photo exhibition, becoming
the first photographer to show his work in public.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.860
One attendee later wrote, “they were like
nothing I’d ever seen… they unite the

00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:29.760
impression of reality with the fantasy of
dreams.”

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.680
Bayard was prepared to demonstrate his process
to the Academy of Sciences, the highest scientific

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.420
authority in France.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.500
And that’s where Francois Arago steps in.

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:44.019
A prominent French politician and astronomer,
and Chair of the Academy of Sciences.

00:02:44.019 --> 00:02:48.720
He was also a supporter of Daguerre's, and
after being shown successful Daguerreotypes

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.140
in late 1838, he set out to arrange a deal
with the French government to purchase the

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:54.930
rights to the invention.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:59.140
He wanted photography to be France’s gift
to the world, and Daguerre was going to be

00:02:59.140 --> 00:03:00.780
the man to deliver it.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.060
So when Bayard showed Arago his successful
prints, the politician persuaded him to hold

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:09.320
off on announcing the details of his method
until the Daguerre deal was finished.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:15.340
And on August 19th, 1839, standing before
the Academy of Sciences with Daguerre seated

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:21.640
right next to him, Francois Arago revealed
the exact process for creating a Daguerreotype.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:24.380
That moment is considered the birth of photography.

00:03:24.380 --> 00:03:28.940
And once the French government made the details
of the process public, Daguerreotyping spread

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:33.780
like wildfire, becoming the first practical,
widespread method of making pictures.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:38.560
Daguerre was awarded a lifetime government
pension, and the principle credit for inventing

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.140
photography — sealing his place in the history
books.

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:47.350
A couple months later, Bayard finally got
his chance with the Academy and demonstrated

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:48.680
his own process.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.260
But it was too late.

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.140
Daguerreotyping was already established, and
the Academy wasn’t interested in pursuing

00:03:54.140 --> 00:03:55.800
his alternative technique.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:59.180
And as a response, Bayard killed himself.

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:01.660
Well, metaphorically.

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:05.620
The tongue-in-cheek caption he wrote on the
back of this self-portrait reads:

00:04:05.629 --> 00:04:10.280
“The corpse which you see here is that of
Monsieur Bayard, inventor of the process that

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:11.780
has just been shown to you.”

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.120
“The government which has been only too
generous to Monsieur Daguerre, has said it

00:04:15.130 --> 00:04:19.510
can do nothing for Monsieur Bayard, and the
poor wretch has drowned himself.”

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:24.460
The image itself is rich with symbols that
Bayard included to hint at his perceived injustice.

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:27.720
First, he posed nude, partially covered in
a shroud,

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.330
a symbol people would have recognized
from iconic paintings depicting martyrs.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:37.220
Another important detail that would have resonated
with 19th century Parisians is in the caption:

00:04:37.220 --> 00:04:42.330
“He has been at the morgue for several days,
and no-one has recognized or claimed him.”

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.110
The Paris Morgue at this time was open every
day to the public, in hopes that people would

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:50.259
claim unidentified bodies — many of them
anonymous suicides.

00:04:50.259 --> 00:04:55.950
Bayard’s protest is all about recognition
– or lack thereof – so the morgue is a

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.040
fitting metaphor.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.700
Just like morgue victims, he’s on display
– propped up and surrounded by personal

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:04.930
effects that might give a clue to the corpse’s
identity.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:07.590
In Bayard’s case, a broad-brimmed straw
hat

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:12.960
and, as a second attempt at the photo shows
more clearly, a floral vase and a statue.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:17.900
Objects that, along with the inventor himself,
were recurring visual themes in his work

00:05:17.900 --> 00:05:21.770
and would have been strong indicators of
the identity of the drowned man,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:25.360
should anyone recognize them, or him, at
all.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:31.960
Bayard went on experimenting with photography
in the years following his “suicide”

00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:36.110
and even became a founding member of the
French Society of Photography.

00:05:36.110 --> 00:05:40.449
But of the many photographs taken over his
long career, it’s this one that is the most

00:05:40.449 --> 00:05:42.100
talked about and analyzed.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.430
And that’s because this isn’t just the
first staged photo – it’s an early example

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.819
of photography showing something non-literal
and symbolic.

00:05:49.819 --> 00:05:53.569
Laying the groundwork for exploring the medium’s
potential for creative expression

00:05:53.569 --> 00:05:58.640
and establishing from the very beginning
that a photograph doesn’t always show the truth.

00:05:59.940 --> 00:06:04.000
